WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
Last time, glutathione took the oxidant hits, and NADPH from this pathway recharged

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.340
it. So why can a plate of fava beans destroy someone's red blood cells?

3
00:00:14.883 --> 00:00:16.863
Here is a mature red blood cell.

4
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:20.543
It has no nucleus and no mitochondria.

5
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:23.083
It needs little ribose.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:28.363
What it needs is NADPH, and it gets it mainly from this pathway.

7
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:35.903
Inside, glutathione peroxidase spends reduced glutathione to neutralize peroxides.

8
00:00:37.243 --> 00:00:41.143
Glutathione reductase then spends NADPH to restore it.

9
00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:45.583
The cycle turns as long as NADPH keeps coming.

10
00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:50.793
So the red cell's whole glutathione cycle leans on one enzyme.

11
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.693
It is G6PD, the committed first step of this pathway.

12
00:00:56.817 --> 00:01:00.037
G6PD deficiency weakens that step.

13
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:06.477
It is the most common human enzyme deficiency, in roughly 400 million people.

14
00:01:07.737 --> 00:01:11.817
The gene sits on the X chromosome, so males are affected more often.

15
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:15.067
Its many variants differ in severity.

16
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:21.667
Then a trigger arrives: fava beans, certain antimalarial or sulfa drugs, or an

17
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:27.387
infection. Each pushes oxidative stress past what a weakened cell can handle.

18
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:31.757
Less NADPH means less reduced glutathione.

19
00:01:32.937 --> 00:01:34.837
Reactive oxygen builds up.

20
00:01:35.937 --> 00:01:38.317
Hemoglobin oxidizes and clumps together.

21
00:01:39.597 --> 00:01:41.297
Those clumps are called Heinz bodies.

22
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:46.870
The damaged cells burst in the circulation, or the spleen pulls them out.

23
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.510
The result is a hemolytic crisis, and a bean started it.

24
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:54.663
Read the chain from start to finish.

25
00:01:55.883 --> 00:01:59.303
Weak G6PD. Less NADPH.

26
00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:02.083
Less reduced glutathione.

27
00:02:03.303 --> 00:02:04.703
More reactive oxygen.

28
00:02:06.063 --> 00:02:07.063
Clumped hemoglobin.

29
00:02:08.523 --> 00:02:09.523
Lost cells.

30
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:16.343
Now a puzzle. A trait this dangerous affects roughly 400 million people.

31
00:02:17.563 --> 00:02:18.823
What could keep it so common?

32
00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:29.037
It is thought to persist because it offers some protection against malaria.

33
00:02:30.437 --> 00:02:35.097
Deficient red cells seem to be a poorer home for the parasite, Plasmodium

34
00:02:35.097 --> 00:02:40.977
falciparum. Where the trait is common broadly overlaps malaria's historical range.

35
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.680
That makes it a balanced polymorphism, like sickle-cell trait.

36
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.080
How strong the protection is, and in whom, still differs between studies.

37
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.120
It is also why doctors screen for it before prescribing certain oxidant drugs.

38
00:02:58.183 --> 00:03:00.363
So one weak step cuts both ways.

39
00:03:01.623 --> 00:03:05.943
It leaves a red cell exposed to a bean, and may leave it a poorer home for a

40
00:03:05.943 --> 00:03:06.943
parasite.

41
00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:12.377
Either way, the red cell's antioxidant shield hangs on a single step.

42
00:03:13.837 --> 00:03:17.817
Weaken G6PD, add an oxidant, and the whole chain gives way.
